 localtext='# Nelson-Atkins Museum of Art — 149 additions verified, 7 October 2026\n\nThe local museum now has **149 linked artworks, all with eligible pre-1971 creation dates**, up from zero accepted linked records. The [applied plan](nelson-reviewed-additions-001-plan.json.gz) adds 149 actual catalogue records in review. This reaches the requested 100–200 range; 51 more eligible records would reach 200. [Readback verification](minimum-100-verification.json) checks every inserted field, identifier, citation and holding assertion. The older pending *Head of an Egyptian* and its two citations remain unchanged. Replay inserted zero rows. No images, painter authorities or links, publication or current-display claims were added.\n\nThe [review register](reviewed-objects-001.csv) and [editorial decisions](editorial-reviewed-001.json.gz) account for all 186 selected object pages: **149 additions, 34 version holds, one existing-identity reconciliation, one group/version hold and one creation-date hold**. Detailed native attributions resolve nine shortened index labels while preserving workshop and tentative attributions. Museum connections have 90% editorial confidence, not a calibrated probability. They do not establish legal title, present custody or current display.\n\nThe [official selection queue](selected-official-queue-001.json) contains 192 distinct indexed leads: 186 selected and six date-screen holds. Bounded European culture filters and American/modern collection pages supplied the selection. A page-two pagination loop produced eight duplicate captures, retained but never counted as distinct works. The six unselected leads include a 1979 painting and five dates needing further parsing or editorial review. The Bassano object’s creation field repeats the artist’s 1510–1592 lifespan and remains held.\n\nAll 186 object extracts are complete. Fifteen long pages have supplementary captures. Reconstruction checks the canonical URL, total line count, exact overlaps, index membership and continuous line coverage. Saved extracts include crawl metadata and original web-tool results; they are **not original object HTTP bytes**. Direct TMS collection requests timed out; the French catalogue returned 403 and 404. Failures remain evidence and were not bypassed. No images were downloaded.\n\nA separate bounded Wikidata query found 261 painting leads. Ten batches captured 200 entities before batch 11 returned HTTP 429. Requests stopped, leaving 61 uncaptured. The [partial capture receipt](partial-secondary-capture-001.json) preserves the error and successful files. Its earlier queue of 173 secondary URLs is discovery evidence, distinct from the active official selection. Secondary multilingual labels and object references assist duplicate searches; they do not replace native facts. Earlier candidate files and both parser supersessions remain intact. [Candidate 003](native-candidates-003.json.gz) correctly separates unspaced signature fields from two literal inventories without changing source text.\n\nThe principal read-only identity scope contains 39,419 artworks, 1,157 artist records and 4,981 aliases, with no exact selected source-URL or native-ID matches. Short-name variants and historical attributions supplement those comparisons. A separate 207-record subject scope checks icons and anonymous portraits. Existing Wtewael *Martyrdom of Saint Sebastian* remains a holding-reconciliation lead. Close-sized portraits, landscapes and still lifes remain held, as do unresolved Canaletto/Bellini versions and the Giaquinto group.\n\nTwo Russian panels, inventories **R96-5/1 and R96-5/2**, retain circa 1490–1510 dates, literal dimensions, Moscow School/Unknown creator labels and Fred Irwig gift provenance. The R prefix is not loan evidence. Rehashed Cypriot sources distinguish their wall paintings from these portable panels. The existing WikiArt El Greco Magdalene source measures 157 × 121 cm, distinct from the selected 101.6 × 81.92 cm canvas; differing dates alone do not prove identity. The small El Greco workshop crucifixion on ebony remains explicitly workshop-attributed.\n\nThe complete Resurrection triptych and the large 32-207 A-P altarpiece each count once. The separately inventoried Bruyn betrothal panels 46-9/1 and 46-9/2 each receive a record, without another record for the pair. Benton’s individually accessioned *American Historical Epic* panels are not supplemented with another whole-ensemble record. A portrait with its case is counted once. Unknown normalized object forms remain null. Source labels, alternate titles, attribution histories, narrative and actual or unknown rights remain citation evidence.\n\nAll **26 Nelson checks** passed within the **788-test campaign suite**, using retained evidence without a test database or catalogue fixtures. Backups are under the configured Artline Library directory. The [wave 49 report](../../verification-after-wave-49.json) freshly verifies 53 addition batches and ten prior holding/date operations. This campaign totals **5,327 new artworks and 765 existing-work museum links**, separate from the unrelated minimum-100 job. **1,198 canonical museums remain below 100 linked records**, so the overall goal remains unfinished.\n'
